<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">French Saint-Christophe</h1><p class="st-meta">1625 CE – 1713 CE · capital Basseterre</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's first lasting colony in the Caribbean, on Saint Kitts, base for the settlement of Guadeloupe and Martinique.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Norman captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-belain-desnambuc">Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc</a> landed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Christopher</a> in 1625 and, with the backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compagnie-de-saint-christophe">Compagnie de Saint-Christophe</a>, founded a French colony. In 1627 the French and English partitioned the island, the French taking the two ends, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basseterre">Basseterre</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>French Saint-Christophe grew tobacco and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, worked by indentured servants and enslaved Africans. From here <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-belain-desnambuc">d'Esnambuc</a> launched the settlement of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-martinique">Martinique</a> in 1635. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phillippe-de-longvilliers-de-poincy">Philippe de Longvilliers de Poincy</a>, governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-antilles">French Antilles</a> from 1639, ruled from a grand château on the island and bought it for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights of Malta</a> in 1651.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The French and English repeatedly expelled each other during the wars of the late 17th century. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> in 1713 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded its share to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, merging it into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Colony of Saint Christopher</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1625 CE – 1713 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.04%;width:1.75%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1625 CE – 1713 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Basseterre</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc, Philippe de Longvilliers de Poincy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Saint Kitts and Nevis</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French Saint Kitts</dd></dl></aside></div>
